  "articleBody": "Dealing with a drug charge is serious enough, but when it escalates to a felony, the stakes get significantly higher. Generally, a drug crime can become a felony based on a few key factors that California law considers. One big one is the type and quantity of the drug involved. Possessing or trafficking larger amounts of certain controlled substances, or particularly dangerous drugs, often pushes a charge into felony territory. Another critical aspect is intent—if the prosecution believes you intended to sell or distribute, rather than just possess for personal use, it's almost always a felony. Prior convictions also play a huge role; even a relatively minor new offense can be charged as a felony if you have a history of drug-related crimes. Plus, if the alleged crime occurred near a school or involved a minor, those aggravating circumstances can elevate charges. If you're facing felony drug charges, you absolutely need a seasoned legal professional. Attorney Angelo J. Reyes has extensive experience with felony drug cases in San Diego County and can help dissect the specifics of your situation.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-26"
}